    <!-- Section 4: Quantization and Memory Optimization -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Weight Quantization: Q4_K_M vs FP16 Tradeoffs</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Downloading model weights over public HTTPS connections requires aggressive weight compression. In Transformers.js v3, models are distributed in three primary formats:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300 text-sm mb-6">
        <li><strong>Q4 (4-bit Block Quantization):</strong> Compresses a 3.8B parameter model from 7.6GB down to ~2.1GB. It fits comfortably in browser storage caches and runs efficiently on laptops with integrated GPUs. Perplexity degradation on general English reasoning is less than 0.8%.</li>
        <li><strong>FP16 (Half Precision):</strong> Preserves 100% of the original parameter precision but doubles memory requirements to ~4.2GB, requiring discrete desktop GPUs with &gt;6GB VRAM.</li>
        <li><strong>OPFS Storage Persistence:</strong> Once downloaded, Transformers.js persists the chunks inside the browser's Origin Private File System (OPFS), which permits zero-copy memory mapping into the WebGPU buffer upon page refresh.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

